<Project>

  <!--
    Applies to every project in the solution, production and test alike.

    RuntimeFrameworkVersion is what turns AC1's ".NET 10.0.11" into a stated, testable
    fact rather than an implication of whichever SDK happens to be installed. global.json
    pins the SDK band; this pins the runtime the output binds to. Gate A asserts both.

    What is NOT here any more: the coding-standard properties. Nullable,
    TreatWarningsAsErrors, ImplicitUsings, EnforceCodeStyleInBuild, AnalysisLevel,
    GenerateDocumentationFile and the CS1591 suppression all moved into
    Opinionated.DotNet.CodingStandards, declared as a GlobalPackageReference in
    Directory.Packages.props. The package sets each of them under a
    `Condition="'$(X)' == ''"` guard and Directory.Build.props is imported first, so
    anything restated here would silently win over the package - which defeats the point
    of having one. Restate a property here only to deliberately override the standard,
    and say why.

    Still true, and now the package's doing rather than this file's: invariants are
    "enforced by construction (type system, single choke point, lint/test gates) rather
    than by an agent remembering to read the spine". Note what is still absent: no
    code-coverage threshold. None appears in the PRD, the spine, the epics or the spec
    kernel, and the 80% figure in the test design is explicitly a proposal. Do not
    invent one.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RuntimeFrameworkVersion>10.0.11</RuntimeFrameworkVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    VALUES MORE THAN ONE PROJECT HAS TO AGREE ON.

    Both of these were previously hard-coded independently in two places with nothing keeping
    the copies equal, and in both cases a divergence was silent rather than loud:

      * The SQL Server image was stated in Yello.AppHost/Program.cs and in
        SqlServerContainerFixture. The suites and local orchestration could therefore run
        different engine builds while every test passed - and AD-15's Latin1_General_100_BIN2
        collation and the row-level security NFR-1 rests on are both engine behaviour.
      * The database resource name was `AddDatabase("yello")` in the AppHost and
        `GetConnectionString("yello")` in the Host. Renaming the resource sent the Host down
        its IsNullOrWhiteSpace branch: it logs a warning, starts normally, and AC4's only
        evidence is gone with the process exit code unchanged.

    They live here because this is the one file every project imports, and reach code as
    assembly metadata rather than as a C# constant. A const would be copied into each consumer
    at compile time - fine within one build, wrong for a value whose entire job is to be the
    single place the fact is stated. Emitted for every project rather than conditioned onto the
    three that read them: the condition would be a second thing to keep in step, which is the
    problem being solved.

    The tag still floats by cumulative update - `2025-latest` is not a digest. That is
    deliberate and remains an open question, revisited when stories 1.5 and 2.6 give a reason
    to freeze the engine. What is settled is that it floats in ONE place.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="Shared values">
    <YelloSqlServerImage>mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2025-latest</YelloSqlServerImage>
    <YelloDatabaseResourceName>yello</YelloDatabaseResourceName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Shared values, readable at runtime">
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="Yello.SqlServerImage" Value="$(YelloSqlServerImage)" />
    <AssemblyMetadata Include="Yello.DatabaseResourceName" Value="$(YelloDatabaseResourceName)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ONE-OFF AUDIT SUPPRESSION - approved by Lee, 2026-10-24.

    System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs 9.0.x arrives transitively via Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
    (and 10.0.8 via the AppHost). No stable SqlClient release fixes it: 7.1.1, the newest, still
    brings 9.0.18 and the fix is 9.0.21. Suppressed by advisory, not by NU1902/NU1903 as a whole,
    so any new advisory still fails the build.

    MessagePack 2.5.302 (GHSA-qhrr-8q5h-9q3h, moderate) arrives transitively via Aspire in the
    AppHost and the Architecture tests that reference it; same one-off, remove when Aspire moves on.

    Remove this ItemGroup when SqlClient ships a fix or Pkcs >= 9.0.21 is in the graph. Tracked
    in deferred-work.md (Pkcs audit entry, owner story 1.10).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="One-off NuGet audit suppressions">
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-846f-f764-76mx" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2fmv-rv2c-59x7" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qhrr-8q5h-9q3h" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Nothing else belongs here. Two ordering facts, both verified against
    `dotnet msbuild -preprocess`, decide where an override has to go:

      * This file is imported BEFORE the package's props, so a property set here wins over
        the package's `Condition="'$(X)' == ''"` default. That is how you override the
        standard - and why restating one of its values here is not a no-op but a silent
        fork of it.
      * The package's own properties are therefore NOT readable here. In particular
        ContinuousIntegrationBuild is set by the package, so a condition on it evaluates
        false in this file no matter the environment. An override that needs to read a
        package property belongs in Directory.Build.targets, which is imported after.
  -->

</Project>
